==== How to Fly: The Glass-Cockpit Cross-Country

A complete cross-country leg in the SR22 G1000, told as one continuous flow rather than a checklist: the screens do the heavy lifting, and you do the thinking. Exact page labels and button names follow the aircraft's own manual — the flow is what matters, and the flow is the same on every leg of your career.

===== The Preflight & Plan

The leg is won before the engine starts, in LittleNavMap. Build the route — departure, destination, the altitudes for the legs, the fuel stop — and then check the weather at both ends: the METARs and TAFs, the wind at the destination, and the ceiling on the arrival. Pick the alternate while you are looking at the map, not while you are looking at the clouds. Export the plan in the sim's format and load it into the aircraft's avionics so the map and the autopilot see exactly the route you planned, and then walk the numbers one last time: fuel on board against trip fuel plus reserve, and the plan legal before the engine turns. The preflight is the cheapest insurance in aviation, and it is the whole wave's lesson in miniature: decide on the ground, fly the decision.

===== Start & Program

With the plan in hand, bring the aircraft to life. Master on, then the start flow — battery, avionics, prime, start — and let the G1000 boot through its self-test while you work: the flight plan page gets the departure, the destination, and the route; the baro/altimeter is set from the local weather; the heading and altitude bugs are set for the departure. Then the check that makes the difference: the MFD's moving map should match the LittleNavMap plan leg for leg, and a mismatch found on the ground costs two minutes, while a mismatch found in the air costs a lot more. The aircraft is ready when the plan is confirmed, not when the engine is warm.

===== Takeoff & Climb

Line up, apply full power, and rotate at the SR22's typical speed, climbing at the book's best-rate speed with the nose pitched for the attitude. Once you are trimmed and climbing, the autopilot earns its keep: altitude select for the cruise altitude, heading select for the departure turn, and then navigation tracking once you are established on the first leg. The phrase to remember is *set and check*: the autopilot holds what you told it, so glance at the map and the groundspeed to confirm you are actually going where the plan thinks you are. The first leg is where the plan becomes a fact, and the check is what makes it yours.

===== Cruise on the Autopilot

The cruise is where a cross-country leg becomes a study in boredom, and boredom is the point. Level at the cruise altitude, manage power and mixture for the book's cruise settings, and know your numbers — the displays show fuel flow and endurance, and the numbers you know before you need them are the numbers that keep you safe. Watch the weather picture as it evolves; if the destination sours, the alternate from the preflight is already known, and the decision is made early or not at all. And use the sim's gift: time warp. Speed up the long, straight cruise and slow down for the work — but remember where you are when you come back, because the leg you are flying is the one you are responsible for.

===== Descent & Arrival

The arrival is the flight's second decision point, and it is made before the airport appears. Plan the descent so you arrive at the destination's pattern or approach altitude in time: the altitude to lose, divided by your descent rate, plus a margin — the math is simple, and it is what keeps an arrival from becoming a rescue. Brief the approach from the plate in AviTab — runway, altitude, frequency, minimums — out loud, because the habit is the skill. Then configure: power back, flaps as the flow demands, speed for the approach, and let the autopilot or your hand fly the final course while you manage the energy. The descent is the moment the whole flight plan converges on the runway, and the pilot who briefed it is the pilot who is calm.

===== Approach & Landing

The landing is the same one you learned in Wave 0, in a faster airplane with more on the line. Line up the aim point — the runway threshold fixed in the windshield — and adjust power for the glide and pitch for the speed; the round-out over the threshold is the same gentle flare, and the mains settle the same way. A crosswind leg means a crab on final and a kick at the flare, and it is the SR22's only secret: practice it, because the ferry routes you fly in this wave will give you plenty of crosswinds. Rollout, brakes to taxi speed, exit at the taxiway, and the leg is yours.

===== The Debrief

Every leg deserves ten minutes of review. Replay the landing in the sim and watch what your hands actually did — a rough touchdown almost always has a visible cause: speed, flare, or staring at the runway instead of the horizon. Check the track against the plan in LittleNavMap: where did you drift, and what would you change? Then log the leg, because every leg is a data point, and Wave 2's instrument work is built on the habit of reviewing what you flew. The ferry pilot's logbook fills up with reviews, not just flights, and that is what makes the next leg better.
